  rankdir="TB";
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This paper proposes to develop and evaluate a graph-neural-network-based Network Flow Compression Model that coarse-grains large complex networks by learning node groupings and minimizing partition-function deviation, with the aim of preserving macroscopic information-flow indicators such as entropy and free energy across propagation scales and network types.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) Across the retained and repaired conclusions, the common pattern is that information-flow preservation in large networks can be operationalized by preserving partition-function behavior, while GNN-based learned representations provide a plausible optimization framework for coarse-graining."];
  "R10" [label="(0,0,0) Preserving the partition-function profile across propagation scale is a suitable criterion for preserving macroscopic information-flow indicators during coarse-graining."];
  "R11" [label="(0,0,0) A graph neural network that produces high-level node representations can be trained using a partition-function-deviation loss so that the learned representations are oriented toward the coarse-graining objective."];
  "R12" [label="(0,0,0) Using structural node features such as degree-related statistics, clustering coefficient, and core number, GNN message passing can construct vector representations for nodes."];
  "R19" [label="(0,0,0) Classical network renormalization methods can be characterized as approaches that compress network size while preserving selected core properties and, in RG settings, often aim to uncover scaling behavior."];
  "R2" [label="(0,0,0) Large networks with role-redundant components motivate investigating coarse-graining strategies that group redundant nodes while explicitly preserving the behavior of interest, including information-flow behavior."];
  "R21" [label="(0,0,0) Training GNNs on many real-world networks motivates testing whether a learned compression model can generalize to unseen datasets and support broad applicability."];
  "R22" [label="(0,0,0) The need for a flow-preserving coarse-graining method, together with a partition-function-deviation loss, motivates developing and evaluating a Network Flow Compression approach for preserving macroscopic information-flow indicators during compression."];
  "R3" [label="(0,0,0) Box-covering provides an example of a network renormalization/coarse-graining lineage aimed at compressing network size while preserving selected core properties."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) Because macroscopic flow indicators can be preserved by preserving the partition-function profile across propagation scale, minimizing deviation from the original partition-function profile is a reasonable optimization proxy for flow-preserving coarse-graining."];
  "R8" [label="(0,0,0) The difficulty of obtaining a closed-form optimal coarse-graining for general complex networks is consistent with considering a data-driven optimization approach that bypasses an analytic solution."];
  "n1" [label="(1,0,0) Currently, complex networks represent many natural and artificial complex systems with heterogeneous connectivity, efficient information transmission, and mesoscale/hierarchical organization."];
  "n15" [label="(17,0,0) Currently, none of these renormalization methods is designed to preserve macroscopic features of information dynamics simultaneously at all propagation scales or for all network types."];
  "n16" [label="(0,0,0) If the research goal is to preserve macroscopic information-dynamics properties across propagation scales and across network types, then existing renormalization methods are insufficient and a new flow-preserving multiscale coarse-graining approach is needed."];
  "n17" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Under the stated goal of preserving macroscopic information-dynamics properties across propagation scales and network types, the fact that existing renormalization/coarse-graining methods are not designed to preserve those features implies that a new flow-preserving multiscale coarse-graining approach is needed for this goal."];
  "n18" [label="(18,0,0) Currently, network density matrices can be used to describe information dynamics in a networked system."];
  "n19" [label="(20,0,0) Currently, macroscopic indicators (e.g., entropy, free energy) can be derived from the network partition function Z_τ, analogously to equilibrium thermodynamics."];
  "n2" [label="(4,0,0) Currently, network science is used to study information flow in many networked systems (e.g., brain signals, epidemics, airline flights, social media messages)."];
  "n20" [label="(0,0,0) abduction-reasoning: Because macroscopic information-flow indicators can be derived from the network partition function Z_tau and network density matrices describe information dynamics, preserving the Z_tau profile across propagation scale is a plausible modeling condition for preserving macroscopic information-flow indicators."];
  "n21" [label="(0,0,0) If macroscopic flow indicators are functions of the partition function Z_τ, then preserving the Z_τ profile across τ is a sufficient condition to preserve those macroscopic flow indicators."];
